WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.867 --> 00:00:02.369
- I mean, think about it.

00:00:02.369 --> 00:00:05.305
We just celebrated Martin
Luther King's birthday,

00:00:05.305 --> 00:00:09.609
a man who is associated
with colorblindness.

00:00:09.609 --> 00:00:11.544 align:start size:88% position:13%
"Judge me by the
content of my character,

00:00:11.544 --> 00:00:13.680
not the color of my skin."

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:16.649
That is what the Civil Rights
Movement used to be about.

00:00:16.649 --> 00:00:18.651
Today, the Civil Rights Movement

00:00:18.651 --> 00:00:21.421
is about Black Lives Matter.

00:00:21.421 --> 00:00:24.424 align:start size:84% position:16%
Not all lives
(laughs), black lives,

00:00:24.424 --> 00:00:27.027
say it, or I'm coming after you.

00:00:27.027 --> 00:00:31.231 align:start size:88% position:13%
We've turned the King
sensibility on its head.

00:00:31.231 --> 00:00:33.233
I mean, that's where
we are as a country.

00:00:33.233 --> 00:00:35.635
And that's what the
left is as a country.

00:00:35.635 --> 00:00:37.337
And I don't think that Joe Biden

00:00:37.337 --> 00:00:39.973
feels he can oppose that

00:00:39.973 --> 00:00:43.209
and succeed politically
in the Democratic Party.

00:00:43.209 --> 00:00:46.413
So I think he's doing what
he feels he needs to do.

00:00:46.413 --> 00:00:49.716 align:start size:88% position:13%
He needed a black woman
as a running mate.

00:00:49.716 --> 00:00:52.052 align:start size:84% position:16%
So he found a
black woman to run.

00:00:52.052 --> 00:00:56.423
And I think he's checking off
the boxes in terms of race

00:00:56.423 --> 00:01:00.093 align:start size:84% position:16%
and gender and
sexual orientation,
right down the list,

00:01:00.093 --> 00:01:02.962
because that is what
today's left demands.

00:01:02.962 --> 00:01:05.632
(bright music)

00:01:09.903 --> 00:01:11.771 align:start size:84% position:16%
- I'm Dave Rubin,
and joining me today

00:01:11.771 --> 00:01:14.574 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
is a senior fellow at
the Manhattan Institute,

00:01:14.574 --> 00:01:16.543 line:0%
a Wall Street Journal columnist

00:01:16.543 --> 00:01:18.378 line:0%
and author of the upcoming book,

00:01:18.378 --> 00:01:21.481 align:start size:84% position:16%
"Maverick: A Biography
of Thomas Sowell."

00:01:21.481 --> 00:01:23.917
Jason Riley, welcome
to "The Rubin Report."

00:01:23.917 --> 00:01:25.251
- Thanks for having me.

00:01:25.251 --> 00:01:26.753
- I am glad to have you here.

00:01:26.753 --> 00:01:30.356
I thought this would be the
right week to have you on,

00:01:30.356 --> 00:01:32.325
not only because the
doc is coming out,

00:01:32.325 --> 00:01:34.761 align:start size:84% position:16%
but obviously
Monday was MLK Day.

00:01:34.761 --> 00:01:36.830
There's a lot going
on in the world,

00:01:36.830 --> 00:01:39.132 align:start size:84% position:16%
and I've been
following what you do

00:01:39.132 --> 00:01:42.001 align:start position:0%
and what the Manhattan Institute
does for quite some time.

00:01:42.001 --> 00:01:43.770 align:start size:84% position:16%
So I'm looking
forward to the chat.

00:01:43.770 --> 00:01:47.040
For the people that don't
have any idea who you are,

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:50.643
who is Jason Riley and why
is he on "The Rubin Report?"

00:01:51.845 --> 00:01:53.847
- (laughs) Well,
I'm a journalist.

00:01:53.847 --> 00:01:57.951 line:0%
I've been affiliated with
the Wall Street Journal

00:01:57.951 --> 00:01:59.452 line:0%
for about a quarter century now.

00:01:59.452 --> 00:02:02.989 align:start size:97% position:3%
And I write a political column
for them, a weekly column,

00:02:02.989 --> 00:02:05.225
mostly about urban issues.

00:02:05.225 --> 00:02:07.927
Crime, immigration, race,

00:02:07.927 --> 00:02:10.964
economics, and so forth.

00:02:10.964 --> 00:02:15.969
And I'm also, like you, a
big fan of Thomas Sowell.

00:02:17.170 --> 00:02:19.072
And I've written a few
books over the years.

00:02:19.072 --> 00:02:23.176
And my most recent one is a
biography of Thomas Sowell,

00:02:23.176 --> 00:02:25.445
which is being published
right around the same time

00:02:25.445 --> 00:02:28.848
that a documentary
film on his life

00:02:28.848 --> 00:02:31.751 align:start size:84% position:16%
that I narrate is
also being released.

00:02:31.751 --> 00:02:34.087 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Yeah, so the book is coming
out actually on May 25th,

00:02:34.087 --> 00:02:35.722 align:start size:84% position:16%
and we'll put the
pre-order link below.

00:02:35.722 --> 00:02:37.957 align:start size:88% position:13%
The doc is actually
coming out this weekend

00:02:37.957 --> 00:02:40.593
on some public TV stations

00:02:40.593 --> 00:02:43.630
and then streaming services
and all that good stuff.

00:02:43.630 --> 00:02:46.432 align:start size:88% position:13%
And obviously, you know,
I know that you know

00:02:46.432 --> 00:02:48.968 align:start size:84% position:16%
how much Thomas Sowell
has affected me,

00:02:48.968 --> 00:02:51.304 align:start size:88% position:13%
and I'm actually honored
to be in the doc.

00:02:51.304 --> 00:02:54.874 align:start size:94% position:6%
And you use some of the
footage from our interview

00:02:54.874 --> 00:02:56.776
that we did up at Stanford.

00:02:56.776 --> 00:02:58.011
But, you know, you
just hit on a bunch

00:02:58.011 --> 00:03:00.113
of the hot topics of the day.

00:03:00.113 --> 00:03:01.681
And as we're taping this today,

00:03:01.681 --> 00:03:04.083 align:start position:0%
it is Joe Biden's inauguration,
but you mentioned,

00:03:04.083 --> 00:03:06.586 align:start size:84% position:16%
you talk about
crime, immigration,

00:03:06.586 --> 00:03:08.755
urban issues, things like that.

00:03:08.755 --> 00:03:10.123 align:start size:84% position:16%
And as a Wall
Street Journal guy,

00:03:10.123 --> 00:03:11.991 align:start size:84% position:16%
my audience can
probably put together

00:03:11.991 --> 00:03:14.594
that lean somewhat right.

00:03:14.594 --> 00:03:17.797
Are you hopeful?
Are you worried?

00:03:17.797 --> 00:03:20.934 align:start size:88% position:13%
What do you feel
about the incoming
administration right now

00:03:20.934 --> 00:03:22.635 align:start size:81% position:19%
on the issues that
you care about?

00:03:23.636 --> 00:03:27.106
- Well, I care about

00:03:27.106 --> 00:03:29.108
how divided we are as
a country right now.

00:03:29.108 --> 00:03:32.312
And I'm hoping that Joe Biden

00:03:32.312 --> 00:03:35.348 align:start size:84% position:16%
can lower the
volume a little bit.

00:03:35.348 --> 00:03:38.251 align:start size:84% position:16%
I think that's gotten
way out of hand

00:03:38.251 --> 00:03:39.719
as we saw as what happened

00:03:39.719 --> 00:03:41.988 align:start size:94% position:6%
at the at the Capitol
there earlier this month.

00:03:41.988 --> 00:03:46.993 align:start size:94% position:6%
So I'm hoping that
that's a priority of his,

00:03:48.161 --> 00:03:50.730
and maybe he'll be
able to do that.

00:03:50.730 --> 00:03:55.134
He's, I'm encouraged
by some of the things

00:03:55.134 --> 00:03:56.869
he's done so far.

00:03:56.869 --> 00:04:01.174
Some of his cabinet picks, I
think, have been quite moderate

00:04:01.174 --> 00:04:02.742
in comparison to what
we could have had.

00:04:02.742 --> 00:04:07.313
We don't have, you know, a
Secretary Warren at Treasury,

00:04:08.781 --> 00:04:12.352
or a Secretary Bernie Sanders
running the labor department.

00:04:12.352 --> 00:04:15.121
So I am hopeful there

00:04:15.121 --> 00:04:17.490 align:start size:88% position:13%
with some of his
choices that he's made.

00:04:17.490 --> 00:04:20.493
On the other hand, you
know, I'm quite disturbed

00:04:20.493 --> 00:04:24.264 align:start position:0%
at the direction of the
Democratic Party in recent years

00:04:24.264 --> 00:04:26.065
with the rise of
the progressives,

00:04:28.101 --> 00:04:31.971
you know, the sort of
woke left disturbs me,

00:04:31.971 --> 00:04:35.475 align:start size:94% position:6%
the Black Lives Matter
movement types disturb me.

00:04:36.609 --> 00:04:39.112 align:start size:88% position:13%
And the fact of the
matter is that Joe Biden

00:04:39.112 --> 00:04:42.982
is the head of a party that's
moved quite a bit to the left

00:04:42.982 --> 00:04:45.218
since the last time he
was in the White House.

00:04:45.218 --> 00:04:47.120
And while he's a
guy who I think is,

00:04:48.288 --> 00:04:50.223
spent a career trying
to find the center

00:04:50.223 --> 00:04:53.159
of the Democratic Party
and stay right there,

00:04:53.159 --> 00:04:55.728
the fact is the party
itself has moved left.

00:04:55.728 --> 00:04:58.831
And so I think we're, I fear
we're going to get a lot more

00:04:58.831 --> 00:05:03.469
of the identity politics that
we had under president Obama,

00:05:03.469 --> 00:05:05.605 align:start size:84% position:16%
and that worries me
because he left office

00:05:05.605 --> 00:05:08.675
with race relations in
pretty, pretty bad shape.

00:05:08.675 --> 00:05:12.578
I think they continued to
sour under President Trump,

00:05:12.578 --> 00:05:16.382
but they were already pretty
bad when Trump got into office

00:05:16.382 --> 00:05:18.651
as a result of what
happened under Obama.

00:05:18.651 --> 00:05:21.988
And I'm fearful that Biden

00:05:21.988 --> 00:05:25.858 align:start size:97% position:3%
and his adherence to identity
politics and so forth

00:05:25.858 --> 00:05:28.695
will keep us going in that
direction, unfortunately.

00:05:28.695 --> 00:05:30.997
- Yeah, are you
kind of surprised

00:05:30.997 --> 00:05:34.000 align:start size:97% position:3%
how much Biden has
embraced identity politics?

00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:37.003 align:start size:94% position:6%
Because as you're pointing
out, he's not actually,

00:05:37.003 --> 00:05:38.971
we think, or at
least his history

00:05:38.971 --> 00:05:41.874
is not one of the sort of
really far left, you know,

00:05:41.874 --> 00:05:45.211 align:start size:97% position:3%
the Bernie Elizabeth Warren's,
it is more of a centrist,

00:05:45.211 --> 00:05:48.381 align:start size:84% position:16%
but it's pretty
clear that, you know,

00:05:48.381 --> 00:05:50.583
he's saying, "Okay, well,
we got Pete Buttigieg,

00:05:50.583 --> 00:05:53.519 align:start size:88% position:13%
so we have a gay
transportation secretary

00:05:53.519 --> 00:05:58.057
and we got a trans, I think,
assistant health secretary

00:05:58.057 --> 00:05:59.826
or deputy health secretary."

00:05:59.826 --> 00:06:02.929
Nobody cares if you have
gay people or trans people

00:06:02.929 --> 00:06:05.998 align:start position:0%
and black people and all of
these things, that's wonderful,

00:06:05.998 --> 00:06:07.967
but of course you
want qualification.

00:06:07.967 --> 00:06:09.435
Are you surprised he's doing,

00:06:09.435 --> 00:06:10.937
or do you think he's just
doing a certain amount

00:06:10.937 --> 00:06:13.373
of placating them to then
get back to the center?

00:06:13.373 --> 00:06:14.874
That would be music to my ears,

00:06:14.874 --> 00:06:18.144 align:start size:94% position:6%
but I fear that energy
that you're talking about.

00:06:18.144 --> 00:06:20.680 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Yeah, I think he
feels he has to do it,

00:06:20.680 --> 00:06:22.915
that this is where the party is.

00:06:24.283 --> 00:06:29.088 align:start size:97% position:3%
and if anything, it's
gotten worse in recent years

00:06:29.956 --> 00:06:31.591
with, I mean, think about it.

00:06:31.591 --> 00:06:35.628
We just celebrated Martin
Luther King's birthday,

00:06:35.628 --> 00:06:39.966 align:start size:88% position:13%
a man who was associated
with colorblindness.

00:06:39.966 --> 00:06:41.901 align:start size:88% position:13%
"Judge me by the
content of my character,

00:06:41.901 --> 00:06:44.036
not the color of my skin."

00:06:44.036 --> 00:06:45.805 align:start size:84% position:16%
That is what the
Civil Rights Movement

00:06:45.805 --> 00:06:47.006
used to be about.

00:06:47.006 --> 00:06:49.008
Today, the Civil Rights Movement

00:06:49.008 --> 00:06:51.744
is about Black Lives Matter.

00:06:51.744 --> 00:06:54.814 align:start size:84% position:16%
Not all lives
(laughs), black lives.

00:06:54.814 --> 00:06:57.116
Say it, or I'm coming after you.

00:06:57.116 --> 00:07:01.754
It we've turned the King
sensibility on its head.

00:07:01.754 --> 00:07:03.556
I mean, that's where
we are as a country.

00:07:03.556 --> 00:07:05.892
And that's what the
left is as a country.

00:07:05.892 --> 00:07:10.229 align:start size:97% position:3%
And I don't think that Joe
Biden feels he can oppose that

00:07:10.229 --> 00:07:13.466
and succeed politically
in the Democratic Party.

00:07:13.466 --> 00:07:16.669
So I think he's doing what
he feels he needs to do.

00:07:16.669 --> 00:07:19.972 align:start size:88% position:13%
He needed a black woman
as a running mate.

00:07:19.972 --> 00:07:22.708 align:start size:84% position:16%
So he found a
black woman to run.

00:07:22.708 --> 00:07:26.646
And I think he's checking off
the boxes in terms of race

00:07:26.646 --> 00:07:30.316 align:start size:84% position:16%
and gender and
sexual orientation,
right down the list,

00:07:30.316 --> 00:07:33.052
because that is what
today's left demands.

00:07:34.086 --> 00:07:35.354
- That's, it's very bizarre.

00:07:35.354 --> 00:07:36.656
I've asked this of
a few other people

00:07:36.656 --> 00:07:38.357
that I've had on the show,

00:07:38.357 --> 00:07:42.462
but are you shocked at the
way these seemingly bad ideas

00:07:42.462 --> 00:07:45.231
have taken a hold of so
many of our institutions

00:07:45.231 --> 00:07:48.901
and our political elite and
our our media institutions

00:07:48.901 --> 00:07:50.269
and everything else?

00:07:50.269 --> 00:07:52.939
- I am, I'm quite concerned.

00:07:52.939 --> 00:07:55.975
and there's so little pushback.

00:07:55.975 --> 00:08:00.913
There's the, even on the left,
they're canceling each other.

00:08:02.281 --> 00:08:05.985 align:start size:94% position:6%
If you don't toe the
line on uncertain issues,

00:08:05.985 --> 00:08:10.356
everyone from a Steven Pinker
or to an Andrew Sullivan to,

00:08:10.356 --> 00:08:12.925 align:start size:97% position:3%
you are not allowed to step
out of line over there,

00:08:12.925 --> 00:08:15.194
or the mob will come after you.

00:08:15.194 --> 00:08:19.232 align:start size:84% position:16%
And I find it
extremely disturbing.

00:08:19.232 --> 00:08:22.802
I don't think that it's helpful

00:08:22.802 --> 00:08:26.372
in terms of addressing
real issues in society.

00:08:26.372 --> 00:08:28.608 align:start size:84% position:16%
It keeps us at each
other's throat,

00:08:28.608 --> 00:08:33.012 align:start size:97% position:3%
but this is how the
Democratic Party wins votes.

00:08:33.012 --> 00:08:36.048
This is how they
drum up their base.

00:08:36.048 --> 00:08:38.818 align:start size:84% position:16%
And what we're going
through right now

00:08:38.818 --> 00:08:40.686
with the critical race theory.

00:08:40.686 --> 00:08:45.124 align:start position:0%
I mean, I'm old enough to
remember when this stuff started

00:08:45.124 --> 00:08:48.461
back in the '70s and
'80s, in particular,

00:08:48.461 --> 00:08:50.496
but it was sort of
limited to academia.

00:08:50.496 --> 00:08:52.598 align:start size:94% position:6%
And now it's spilled
over into the mainstream,

00:08:52.598 --> 00:08:56.569
and we're talking about,
you know, unconscious bias

00:08:56.569 --> 00:08:59.739 align:start size:97% position:3%
and these ridiculous
diversity training programs

00:08:59.739 --> 00:09:03.142 align:start size:88% position:13%
and demanding the
self-abasement of whites

00:09:03.142 --> 00:09:06.012
and to acknowledge their
privilege and all the rest.

00:09:06.012 --> 00:09:08.748
This is, I think this is
horrible for the country.

00:09:08.748 --> 00:09:10.983
Just absolutely horrible.

00:09:10.983 --> 00:09:13.586
And I think all of
that will be ascended

00:09:13.586 --> 00:09:15.888 align:start size:94% position:6%
in a Joe Biden
presidency, unfortunately.

00:09:15.888 --> 00:09:17.390
- Yeah, I'm with you.

00:09:17.390 --> 00:09:20.560
And unfortunately is
the key word there.

00:09:20.560 --> 00:09:22.361 align:start size:84% position:16%
So you mentioned just
sort of generally

00:09:22.361 --> 00:09:24.130 align:start position:0%
the state of race relations
that they got worse under Obama.

00:09:24.130 --> 00:09:25.665
You feel they got
worse under Trump.

00:09:25.665 --> 00:09:28.334 align:start size:84% position:16%
And now here we are
with this new ideology

00:09:28.334 --> 00:09:30.269
that seemingly will
only make it worse.

00:09:30.269 --> 00:09:32.305
When you say got worse, what,

00:09:32.305 --> 00:09:33.773
how do you actually define that?

00:09:33.773 --> 00:09:35.408
Because I find when I,

00:09:35.408 --> 00:09:37.543 align:start size:97% position:3%
the limited amount that I've
traveled in the last year

00:09:37.543 --> 00:09:40.012 align:start size:94% position:6%
because of COVID, but from
talking to my audience

00:09:40.012 --> 00:09:41.380
and hearing what
people are saying

00:09:41.380 --> 00:09:43.015
and when I do get to travel,

00:09:43.015 --> 00:09:45.585
I think most people don't
really care about race.

00:09:45.585 --> 00:09:48.120 align:start size:97% position:3%
I don't think people really
care about sexuality.

00:09:48.120 --> 00:09:50.056
I certainly don't think
they care about gender.

00:09:50.056 --> 00:09:53.960
I think Americans, probably
something like 80% of them

00:09:53.960 --> 00:09:55.261
are kind of live and let live.

00:09:55.261 --> 00:09:56.796
They just don't know it exactly.

00:09:56.796 --> 00:09:58.331
It's not the messaging they get.

00:09:58.331 --> 00:10:00.900
How do you actually
grade sort of, okay,

00:10:00.900 --> 00:10:04.804
it got worse under Obama or
it got worse under Trump?

00:10:04.804 --> 00:10:07.340
- Well, I'm basing
that on polling data.

00:10:07.340 --> 00:10:10.610 align:start size:94% position:6%
Gallup Poll ask this
question every few years.

00:10:10.610 --> 00:10:13.913
Do you think race relations
have improved or worsened?

00:10:13.913 --> 00:10:18.484 align:start size:84% position:16%
And when Obama left
office in 2015,

00:10:18.484 --> 00:10:21.754
or 2016, I should say,

00:10:21.754 --> 00:10:26.359 align:start size:88% position:13%
that poll answer showed
that race relations

00:10:26.359 --> 00:10:30.563 align:start size:94% position:6% line:0%
were at their lowest point
since the early '90s.

00:10:30.563 --> 00:10:32.465 line:0%
So they had really,
really tapered off,

00:10:32.465 --> 00:10:34.967 align:start size:94% position:6% line:0%
and then they didn't
improve much under Trump,

00:10:34.967 --> 00:10:37.603
at least in terms of how
people were responding

00:10:37.603 --> 00:10:39.071
to that question.

00:10:39.071 --> 00:10:42.408 align:start size:97% position:3%
Now, there could be something
in the way the question

00:10:42.408 --> 00:10:45.177
is asked frame or something,

00:10:45.177 --> 00:10:50.149
and maybe anecdotally, it's
easy to counter that notion.

00:10:51.550 --> 00:10:54.387 align:start size:97% position:3%
I mean, if, you know,
intermarriage rates are up,

00:10:54.387 --> 00:10:59.058 align:start size:97% position:3%
(laughs) you know, if things
like that are going on

00:10:59.058 --> 00:11:00.526
if there's less segregation

00:11:00.526 --> 00:11:04.530 align:start size:84% position:16%
in residential
housing and so forth,

00:11:04.530 --> 00:11:07.566
that would all point in
the opposite direction.

00:11:07.566 --> 00:11:09.702
So it may be a way of how
the question is framed,

00:11:09.702 --> 00:11:11.070
but I'm basing it
on the polling,

00:11:11.070 --> 00:11:12.571
the polling data that I've read.

00:11:12.571 --> 00:11:13.906
- Yeah, and then
at the same time,

00:11:13.906 --> 00:11:15.307
we see interesting things.

00:11:15.307 --> 00:11:17.576
Like, I think, it was
black female support,

00:11:17.576 --> 00:11:21.614 align:start size:94% position:6%
I think, doubled for Trump
than four years prior,

00:11:21.614 --> 00:11:23.149
we saw the Brexit movement.

00:11:23.149 --> 00:11:26.719
We did see some markers that
things are kind of changing,

00:11:26.719 --> 00:11:30.623
but I sense now it
could all be for not.

00:11:30.623 --> 00:11:34.660 align:start size:84% position:16%
- I think we saw
some indications that
there's no reason

00:11:34.660 --> 00:11:38.197
for the Republican Party to
give up on the minority vote.

00:11:38.197 --> 00:11:42.368 align:start size:94% position:6%
Trump improved his
performance among blacks,

00:11:42.368 --> 00:11:45.304 align:start size:84% position:16%
and especially among
Hispanic voters.

00:11:45.304 --> 00:11:49.475
And I think that had a
lot to do with his focus

00:11:49.475 --> 00:11:52.845 align:start size:84% position:16%
on reopening the
economy after COVID.

00:11:52.845 --> 00:11:54.880 align:start size:88% position:13%
People forget how bad
things were particularly

00:11:54.880 --> 00:11:58.417
for black men under Obama
in terms of employment.

00:11:58.417 --> 00:12:01.821
The black unemployment rate did
not fall below double digits

00:12:01.821 --> 00:12:04.990
until the seventh year
of the Obama presidency.

00:12:04.990 --> 00:12:08.561
So things were very bad
for a very long time.

00:12:08.561 --> 00:12:12.498
And then under Trump, we see
record low unemployment rates,

00:12:12.498 --> 00:12:14.400
record low poverty rates.

00:12:14.400 --> 00:12:18.003
We see the wages rising for
the least skilled workers

00:12:18.003 --> 00:12:20.606 align:start size:84% position:16%
at the fastest rate
of all workers.

00:12:20.606 --> 00:12:22.842 align:start size:84% position:16%
Things were going
quite well pre-COVID

00:12:22.842 --> 00:12:24.343
in terms of the economy,

00:12:25.478 --> 00:12:28.080
and COVID really
shut all that down.

00:12:28.080 --> 00:12:30.983
And I think Trump's
focus on reopening

00:12:30.983 --> 00:12:32.885
and getting people back to work

00:12:32.885 --> 00:12:36.922 align:start size:97% position:3%
is one reason he improved
his numbers among minorities.

00:12:36.922 --> 00:12:39.258
- Yeah, so before we
jump into to the doc

00:12:39.258 --> 00:12:41.727
and Thomas Sowell and all that,

00:12:41.727 --> 00:12:44.463 align:start size:94% position:6%
let's do a little bit
the right side of things.

00:12:45.698 --> 00:12:47.967
I think a lot of people
that are watching this

00:12:47.967 --> 00:12:49.401 align:start size:84% position:16%
probably agreeing
with your assessment

00:12:49.401 --> 00:12:50.870
of what's going on on the left.

00:12:50.870 --> 00:12:52.905 align:start position:0%
What do you make of what's
going on on the right right now?

00:12:52.905 --> 00:12:54.173
Like do you think Trump

00:12:54.173 --> 00:12:56.408
is gonna lead some sort
of Patriot Party now?

00:12:56.408 --> 00:12:59.211 align:start size:94% position:6%
What happens to
establishment Republicans?

00:12:59.211 --> 00:13:01.714
Can they reconcile
all of this stuff?

00:13:03.115 --> 00:13:06.585
- Well, who knows what
Donald Trump is gonna do?

00:13:06.585 --> 00:13:07.419
(laughs) I mean.

00:13:08.387 --> 00:13:10.022
- Indeed, indeed.

00:13:10.022 --> 00:13:13.759
- I've stopped trying to
guess about his intentions.

00:13:13.759 --> 00:13:15.861
It may be limited
in what he can do,

00:13:15.861 --> 00:13:20.099 align:start size:97% position:3%
depending on how this
impeachment process plays out.

00:13:20.099 --> 00:13:23.469
I guess Mitch McConnell
has made some comments

00:13:23.469 --> 00:13:27.840
that lead some to believe that
he would vote for conviction.

00:13:27.840 --> 00:13:32.845 align:start size:97% position:3%
And others say that if he
does, others will follow suit.

00:13:33.979 --> 00:13:38.150 align:start size:84% position:16%
And if the Senate
convicts him in a way

00:13:38.150 --> 00:13:40.920
that prevents him from
running for office again,

00:13:40.920 --> 00:13:42.955
then that could quash
efforts going forward.

00:13:42.955 --> 00:13:44.757 align:start size:84% position:16%
I guess he could
still have influence

00:13:44.757 --> 00:13:48.093
in terms of endorsing
people or so forth

00:13:48.093 --> 00:13:53.065
or funding movements, but
in terms of personally,

00:13:53.065 --> 00:13:54.600
and his own political career,

00:13:54.600 --> 00:13:58.504 align:start size:97% position:3%
that could probably be the the
nail in the coffin on that.

00:13:58.504 --> 00:14:00.673 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Yeah, it does
seem like, you know,

00:14:00.673 --> 00:14:02.474
the quote-unquote swamp

00:14:02.474 --> 00:14:05.711
is sort of ready to just
circle and expunge him,

00:14:05.711 --> 00:14:09.048
but what do you do with
those 74 million people?

00:14:09.048 --> 00:14:10.282 align:start size:84% position:16%
I guess that's
really the question.

00:14:10.282 --> 00:14:13.085
- Well, yeah. He's,
got his followers.

00:14:13.085 --> 00:14:14.453 align:start size:84% position:16%
We don't know what
will happen with them.

00:14:14.453 --> 00:14:15.788
Some of them were people

00:14:15.788 --> 00:14:17.489 align:start size:94% position:6%
that were not politically
active at all.

00:14:17.489 --> 00:14:20.759
He made them come out

00:14:20.759 --> 00:14:22.528 align:start size:97% position:3%
and participate in politics
for the first time.

00:14:22.528 --> 00:14:26.165 align:start size:88% position:13%
So could they go back
to being inactive again?

00:14:26.165 --> 00:14:27.600
Some of them, a lot of them,

00:14:27.600 --> 00:14:29.034 align:start size:94% position:6%
and the left doesn't
like to acknowledge this,

00:14:29.034 --> 00:14:31.170 align:start size:84% position:16%
but a lot of them were
former Obama voters

00:14:31.170 --> 00:14:33.339
that he won in 2016.

00:14:33.339 --> 00:14:36.709 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Me!
- And held onto in 2020.

00:14:36.709 --> 00:14:39.311 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Yeah.
- So what will become

00:14:39.311 --> 00:14:42.047
of those voters without Trump?

00:14:42.047 --> 00:14:44.183
But, you know, yes.

00:14:44.183 --> 00:14:45.651
You know, the Republican Party

00:14:45.651 --> 00:14:48.787 align:start size:97% position:3%
is worried about losing
some of those Trump voters,

00:14:48.787 --> 00:14:50.289
but I also think,

00:14:50.289 --> 00:14:53.626 align:start size:97% position:3%
and I don't know how they
compare in terms of numbers,

00:14:53.626 --> 00:14:56.896
but I can say there are also
a fair number of Republicans

00:14:56.896 --> 00:14:58.631
like my wife, for instance,

00:14:58.631 --> 00:15:01.333 align:start size:97% position:3%
who could never bring herself
to support Donald Trump.

00:15:01.333 --> 00:15:03.335
She didn't like the way
he talked about women

00:15:03.335 --> 00:15:07.740
or demean the war record of
military heroes and so forth.

00:15:07.740 --> 00:15:11.977 align:start size:84% position:16%
So she did not support
Republicans while
he was in office,

00:15:11.977 --> 00:15:13.579
but she's perfectly
ready to go back

00:15:13.579 --> 00:15:16.081 align:start size:97% position:3%
to supporting Republican
presidential candidates now.

00:15:16.081 --> 00:15:18.884
So I don't know how
representative she is

00:15:18.884 --> 00:15:20.486
of people out there.

00:15:20.486 --> 00:15:25.424
So who knows how
this will play out.

00:15:25.424 --> 00:15:28.961 align:start size:84% position:16%
But I think it's
clear that the country

00:15:28.961 --> 00:15:31.597
may have been tired
of Donald Trump,

00:15:31.597 --> 00:15:34.366
but they voted for
divided government.

00:15:34.366 --> 00:15:38.637 align:start size:94% position:6%
They shrunk the Democrats
lead in the House.

00:15:38.637 --> 00:15:40.239
We have a 50/50 Senate.

00:15:41.640 --> 00:15:46.111 align:start size:94% position:6%
So the country is not
throwing out republicanism

00:15:46.111 --> 00:15:47.613
with Donald Trump.

00:15:47.613 --> 00:15:50.749 align:start size:97% position:3%
And I think that's something
Biden should keep in mind

00:15:50.749 --> 00:15:53.185
as he goes forward governing.

00:15:53.185 --> 00:15:56.088
- Yeah, I mean, in
my humble opinion,

00:15:56.088 --> 00:15:58.324 align:start size:88% position:13%
if he goes down the
identity politics route

00:15:58.324 --> 00:15:59.825
that it seems like
he's going down,

00:15:59.825 --> 00:16:02.294 align:start size:97% position:3%
they're going to get crushed
even harder in two years,

00:16:02.294 --> 00:16:05.397
which probably would be good
to balance things out, but.

00:16:05.397 --> 00:16:08.167
- Well, I think, you know,

00:16:08.167 --> 00:16:10.970
Obama made this mistake
when he came into office,

00:16:10.970 --> 00:16:12.671
and maybe Joe Biden
learned from it,

00:16:12.671 --> 00:16:16.175
but I think they
saw George W. Bush

00:16:16.175 --> 00:16:18.911 align:start size:97% position:3%
with very low approval
ratings going out of office.

00:16:18.911 --> 00:16:20.980 align:start size:84% position:16%
And they interpreted
that as a mandate

00:16:20.980 --> 00:16:22.247
for a far left agenda.

00:16:22.247 --> 00:16:23.615
So they passed the
stimulus package.

00:16:23.615 --> 00:16:25.117
They didn't do much.

00:16:25.117 --> 00:16:28.721 align:start size:94% position:6%
And then they turned their
attention to reforming,

00:16:28.721 --> 00:16:31.290 align:start size:97% position:3%
transforming the healthcare
system in this country

00:16:31.290 --> 00:16:33.993
in a highly partisan fashion.

00:16:33.993 --> 00:16:35.694
And it turned out to
be hugely unpopular.

00:16:35.694 --> 00:16:38.430 align:start size:84% position:16%
They lost in time both
houses of Congress,

00:16:38.430 --> 00:16:41.333 align:start size:97% position:3%
the Democrats did, and it
turned out to be a disaster.

00:16:41.333 --> 00:16:43.068
And it'll be interesting to know

00:16:43.068 --> 00:16:45.137
if Biden interprets
Trump's unpopularity

00:16:45.137 --> 00:16:48.807
with a mandate for
moving forward with
a progressive agenda.

00:16:48.807 --> 00:16:53.612
I don't think people voted for
him for a progressive agenda.

00:16:53.612 --> 00:16:57.483 align:start size:84% position:16%
So we'll see if Joe
Biden learned anything

00:16:57.483 --> 00:16:59.351
from the last time he
was in the White House.

00:16:59.351 --> 00:17:00.986 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Yeah, that I guess
is the tension.

00:17:00.986 --> 00:17:02.855
It's like, yeah, he wants to,

00:17:02.855 --> 00:17:05.858 align:start size:97% position:3%
or maybe in the past, would
have been more center.

00:17:05.858 --> 00:17:07.359
The energy's over here,

00:17:07.359 --> 00:17:10.329
and just sort of how far
can that push and pull go.

00:17:10.329 --> 00:17:11.563
All right.

00:17:11.563 --> 00:17:13.499
So let's talk about the
doc and Thomas Sowell.

00:17:13.499 --> 00:17:15.000
First off,

00:17:15.000 --> 00:17:17.503
when did you become aware of
the work of Thomas Sowell?

00:17:17.503 --> 00:17:20.039 align:start size:84% position:16%
How did this whole
project come together?

00:17:21.440 --> 00:17:25.210 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Oh, well, I first became
aware of Thomas Sowell

00:17:25.210 --> 00:17:28.247
when I was in college
in the early 1990s.

00:17:28.247 --> 00:17:31.850
I was having some, where I
worked in my college paper,

00:17:31.850 --> 00:17:36.188 align:start position:0%
and I was having a conversation
about Affirmative Action

00:17:36.188 --> 00:17:40.893 align:start size:94% position:6%
with one of my colleagues
on the school paper.

00:17:40.893 --> 00:17:42.327
And another person
walked over and said,

00:17:42.327 --> 00:17:43.996
"You sound like Tom Sowell."

00:17:43.996 --> 00:17:45.697
And I said, "Tom, who?"

00:17:45.697 --> 00:17:49.101
And I had no idea who he was.

00:17:49.101 --> 00:17:52.137
Person wrote down
the name of a book.

00:17:52.137 --> 00:17:55.107
I went and checked it out of
school library that evening

00:17:55.107 --> 00:17:56.442
and read it in one sitting

00:17:56.442 --> 00:17:59.078
and spent the weekend
reading everything else

00:17:59.078 --> 00:18:01.046 align:start size:84% position:16%
that the library had
by Thomas Sowell.

00:18:01.046 --> 00:18:02.548
And I was hooked.

00:18:02.548 --> 00:18:05.684
And so he really
had a big impact

00:18:05.684 --> 00:18:10.622
on my sort of intellectual
development since college,

00:18:10.622 --> 00:18:13.659 align:start size:94% position:6%
but I was already
leaning more conservative

00:18:13.659 --> 00:18:15.461
by the time I discovered Thomas,

00:18:15.461 --> 00:18:18.664 align:start size:97% position:3%
which is, I found out later,
is not at all uncommon.

00:18:18.664 --> 00:18:20.299 align:start size:84% position:16%
I mean, by the time
Clarence Thomas

00:18:20.299 --> 00:18:23.602 align:start size:94% position:6%
discovered Thomas
Sowell, his reaction was,

00:18:23.602 --> 00:18:26.472 align:start size:97% position:3%
"Wow, there's someone else
out there who thinks like me."

00:18:26.472 --> 00:18:28.574
Walter Williams who
recently passed away

00:18:28.574 --> 00:18:30.876
had the exact same experience.

00:18:30.876 --> 00:18:33.045
They were friends for 50 years.

00:18:33.045 --> 00:18:35.147
And, but by, when
they first met,

00:18:35.147 --> 00:18:38.016
Walter Williams was already
Walter Williams (laughs).

00:18:38.951 --> 00:18:42.087
So you hear this time and again,

00:18:42.087 --> 00:18:44.723
people have found their
way to Thomas Sowell,

00:18:44.723 --> 00:18:47.659 align:start size:94% position:6%
but he hasn't necessarily
led them to believe

00:18:47.659 --> 00:18:50.129
what they now believe.

00:18:50.129 --> 00:18:52.764
- Right, so as a guy that
writing a book about him,

00:18:52.764 --> 00:18:54.366
or wrote a book about
him that's upcoming

00:18:54.366 --> 00:18:56.502
and did the doc and everything,

00:18:56.502 --> 00:18:58.437
you must be just
like over the moon

00:18:58.437 --> 00:19:00.706
that he's had this resurgence,

00:19:00.706 --> 00:19:03.509
this renaissance
with young people.

00:19:03.509 --> 00:19:06.345 align:start size:88% position:13%
Because when I
interviewed him, I mean,

00:19:06.345 --> 00:19:09.448 align:start size:84% position:16%
the amount of
emails that we got,

00:19:09.448 --> 00:19:12.451
and handwritten letters
and Facebook messages

00:19:12.451 --> 00:19:17.456
and the whole thing, saying
this man transformed my life.

00:19:18.891 --> 00:19:20.392
And in many ways for me, you
know, people always say to me,

00:19:20.392 --> 00:19:22.094
"Dave, it seems like you've
changed so much politically."

00:19:22.094 --> 00:19:25.063
And I always say, "Well,
actually, mostly the same.

00:19:25.063 --> 00:19:27.399
Economics is where I changed.

00:19:27.399 --> 00:19:31.069
It's just, that's the one,
like I don't deny it," right?

00:19:31.069 --> 00:19:33.839
But the other stuff
is pretty much there

00:19:33.839 --> 00:19:35.040
and was always there.

00:19:35.040 --> 00:19:36.542 align:start size:88% position:13%
But economics is the
one that really changed.

00:19:36.542 --> 00:19:38.010
And it was because
of Thomas Sowell.

00:19:38.010 --> 00:19:40.445 align:start size:84% position:16%
It was happening
before that interview.

00:19:40.445 --> 00:19:43.215 align:start size:84% position:16%
But to me that
moment, that was it,

00:19:43.215 --> 00:19:45.417
that pretty much was it.

00:19:45.417 --> 00:19:49.154
- And I am thrilled that a
younger generation of people

00:19:49.154 --> 00:19:52.758
are interested in Sowell
because what he is writing,

00:19:52.758 --> 00:19:55.894
written over the years is
as relevant today as ever.

00:19:55.894 --> 00:19:58.497
He was writing about
social justice issues

00:19:58.497 --> 00:20:00.632
30 and 40 years ago.

00:20:00.632 --> 00:20:04.937
And he's still right
about these things.

00:20:04.937 --> 00:20:07.472
In fact, it is amazing how long

00:20:07.472 --> 00:20:10.943
he has been so right
about so many issues.

00:20:10.943 --> 00:20:13.011
You can go back and
read personal letters

00:20:13.011 --> 00:20:15.280
that he wrote in
the 1960s to people

00:20:15.280 --> 00:20:17.349 align:start size:88% position:13%
about how he thought the
Civil Tights Movement

00:20:17.349 --> 00:20:18.283
was going to end.

00:20:19.284 --> 00:20:22.554 line:0%
It's really, really amazing how,

00:20:22.554 --> 00:20:24.990 line:0%
his prescience in that regard,

00:20:24.990 --> 00:20:29.761 align:start size:97% position:3% line:0%
but so in terms of me
personally discovering Sowell,

00:20:29.761 --> 00:20:31.897 align:start size:88% position:13%
that's what had happened
back in college.

00:20:31.897 --> 00:20:33.899
In terms of the film.

00:20:33.899 --> 00:20:36.134 align:start size:84% position:16%
Well, I guess I should
start with a book.

00:20:36.134 --> 00:20:40.639 align:start size:97% position:3%
I've been, I've known Sowell
for about 15, 20 years,

00:20:40.639 --> 00:20:42.140
and I've been pressuring him

00:20:42.140 --> 00:20:46.245 align:start size:88% position:13%
to let me do this
biography for some time.

00:20:46.245 --> 00:20:48.380
And I knew I could
do it without him.

00:20:48.380 --> 00:20:49.881 align:start size:81% position:19%
He said I could
do it without him,

00:20:49.881 --> 00:20:51.350
but I wanted his cooperation.

00:20:51.350 --> 00:20:54.419 align:start size:97% position:3%
I didn't think a lot of
other people would talk to me

00:20:54.419 --> 00:20:56.922
if they, if I didn't have
his blessing to do it.

00:20:56.922 --> 00:21:00.592
And he finally gave in and let
me start restart writing it.

00:21:00.592 --> 00:21:02.761
So I started on it
about five years ago.

00:21:02.761 --> 00:21:06.131 align:start size:97% position:3%
Four years ago, I got
interrupted by someone else

00:21:06.131 --> 00:21:07.799
to write another book
about another issue.

00:21:07.799 --> 00:21:09.635
So I put the Sowell book aside,

00:21:09.635 --> 00:21:12.237
and then I got back
to it about 2018.

00:21:12.237 --> 00:21:16.541
And the results will be
out in the in the spring.

00:21:16.541 --> 00:21:19.911
When the people at the
Free To Choose network,

00:21:19.911 --> 00:21:22.047
and they're the ones who
produce the documentary,

00:21:22.047 --> 00:21:23.415
learned about the book,

00:21:23.415 --> 00:21:24.950
they came to me and asked me

00:21:24.950 --> 00:21:27.085
if I would be interested in
narrating a documentary film

00:21:27.085 --> 00:21:28.720
that they were putting together.

00:21:28.720 --> 00:21:30.789
And Sowell had a long history

00:21:30.789 --> 00:21:34.159 align:start position:0%
with the Free To Choose network
through Milton Friedman,

00:21:34.159 --> 00:21:37.095
one of his mentors who did
a series for Free To Choose

00:21:37.095 --> 00:21:40.232 align:start size:88% position:13%
on public television
back in the early 1980s,

00:21:40.232 --> 00:21:43.268
and Sowell was, participated
in Milton Friedman's show.

00:21:43.268 --> 00:21:46.471
And so they wanted to do
a documentary on Sowell.

00:21:46.471 --> 00:21:48.840 align:start size:97% position:3%
They asked me if I was
interested in being a narrator

00:21:48.840 --> 00:21:50.609 align:start size:84% position:16%
and helping and
developing the film.

00:21:50.609 --> 00:21:52.744
And that's how that got started.

00:21:52.744 --> 00:21:54.379
- Yeah, there's a moment.

00:21:54.379 --> 00:21:56.782 align:start position:0%
I think when I was interviewed,
I mentioned this moment.

00:21:56.782 --> 00:21:58.383
I don't know if it
made it into the doc,

00:21:58.383 --> 00:21:59.985
but I'm sure you'll
appreciate this.

00:21:59.985 --> 00:22:02.621
When I finished the
interview with him,

00:22:02.621 --> 00:22:05.290
and I had tweeted out
right before, you know,

00:22:05.290 --> 00:22:07.726
I'm about to sit down
with Thomas Sowell.

00:22:07.726 --> 00:22:09.494 align:start size:94% position:6%
You know, if he's
influenced you in any way,

00:22:09.494 --> 00:22:10.696
send me a message.

00:22:10.696 --> 00:22:11.797 align:start size:84% position:16%
And maybe I can read
a couple to him.

00:22:11.797 --> 00:22:12.898
And I got, I mean,

00:22:14.066 --> 00:22:15.300
I got hundreds if not
thousands of tweets.

00:22:15.300 --> 00:22:17.069
And I said to him
at the end, I said,

00:22:17.069 --> 00:22:19.938 align:start size:84% position:16%
"Dr. Sowell, I tweeted
out that, you know,

00:22:19.938 --> 00:22:21.173
I'm sitting down with you,

00:22:21.173 --> 00:22:22.708
and I just got so many
of these great messages.

00:22:22.708 --> 00:22:23.875
If you want to take a look."

00:22:23.875 --> 00:22:25.410 align:start size:84% position:16%
And I kind of
showed him my phone,

00:22:25.410 --> 00:22:26.978
and he goes with that
great voice, he goes,

00:22:26.978 --> 00:22:31.683 align:start size:97% position:3%
"Young man, I've been doing
this for a long time."

00:22:31.683 --> 00:22:34.720 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I just thought it
was the perfect response.

00:22:34.720 --> 00:22:36.588
Like truly the perfect response.

00:22:36.588 --> 00:22:38.056
Getting to what you're saying,

00:22:38.056 --> 00:22:42.294 align:start position:0%
that the things that he was
talking about 30, 40 years ago,

00:22:42.294 --> 00:22:45.063
they're relevant and
feel new to us now.

00:22:45.063 --> 00:22:48.233 align:start size:88% position:13%
But this has been a
long slog for this guy.

00:22:48.233 --> 00:22:50.702
- Oh yeah. Yeah, it has been.

00:22:50.702 --> 00:22:55.674
And there's a, lot of people
think that Tom is on Twitter

00:22:56.575 --> 00:22:59.945
because there is a fan site,

00:22:59.945 --> 00:23:04.850 align:start size:97% position:3%
and it's run by a millennial
who has never met Tom.

00:23:05.717 --> 00:23:06.918
It's not an authorized side.

00:23:06.918 --> 00:23:09.287 align:start size:84% position:16%
And all this guy
does is tweet quotes

00:23:09.287 --> 00:23:11.523
from past Sowell
columns and books.

00:23:11.523 --> 00:23:14.259 align:start size:94% position:6%
That's it, there's no
added commentary to them.

00:23:14.259 --> 00:23:17.529 align:start size:94% position:6%
He just takes these quotes
and tweets them out.

00:23:17.529 --> 00:23:20.365 align:start size:88% position:13%
It has hundreds of
thousands of followers,

00:23:20.365 --> 00:23:21.900 align:start size:84% position:16%
which would be
amazing for anyone,

00:23:21.900 --> 00:23:25.070
but especially for someone
who's not on social media.

00:23:25.070 --> 00:23:26.238
(laughs) like Thomas Sowell is.

00:23:26.238 --> 00:23:28.540 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Yeah. No, it
really, that account.

00:23:28.540 --> 00:23:29.374
I mean, it's at Thomas Sowell.

00:23:29.374 --> 00:23:30.876
And for a long time,

00:23:30.876 --> 00:23:32.377 align:start size:94% position:6%
I thought it was some
sort of official account,

00:23:32.377 --> 00:23:35.414
but every time they tweet
out a meme or just a quote,

00:23:35.414 --> 00:23:37.182
whatever it is, and it's like
if he wrote that yesterday,

00:23:37.182 --> 00:23:39.718 align:start size:94% position:6%
you'd be going, "Man,
this guy knows what's up."

00:23:39.718 --> 00:23:42.120
And then of course you find
out, oh, no, he wrote that,

00:23:42.120 --> 00:23:45.056
you know, in 1969 or
something like that.

00:23:45.056 --> 00:23:46.825 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Well, people are,
what's amazing.

00:23:46.825 --> 00:23:48.126
And this speaks to your comment

00:23:48.126 --> 00:23:51.363
about how long Sowell
has been doing this.

00:23:51.363 --> 00:23:56.368 align:start size:97% position:3%
He, you know, people often say
to me after I give a speech

00:23:57.469 --> 00:23:59.037
or write something in the paper,

00:23:59.037 --> 00:24:00.972
"Oh, you know, he
must be very brave

00:24:00.972 --> 00:24:02.174
to write something like that."

00:24:02.174 --> 00:24:03.909 align:start size:81% position:19%
You know, and it's
really easily,

00:24:03.909 --> 00:24:06.511
if I write something
contrarian about race

00:24:06.511 --> 00:24:08.313
or an issue like that.

00:24:08.313 --> 00:24:10.849
And I don't think
there's anything

00:24:10.849 --> 00:24:12.350
particularly brave about it.

00:24:12.350 --> 00:24:14.920 align:start size:97% position:3%
I think of Sowell as someone
who's particularly brave.

00:24:14.920 --> 00:24:17.222
I mean, he's been doing
this for a long time.

00:24:17.222 --> 00:24:19.090 align:start size:81% position:19%
Well, before there
was a Fox News.

00:24:19.090 --> 00:24:21.560
Well before there was
social media support

00:24:21.560 --> 00:24:23.895 align:start size:84% position:16%
or talk radio people
that had his back

00:24:23.895 --> 00:24:25.130
and that sort of thing.

00:24:25.130 --> 00:24:26.998
He was just out there
writing this stuff,

00:24:26.998 --> 00:24:29.100
and he was doing
it largely alone.

00:24:29.100 --> 00:24:31.803
And without the support
that many conservatives,

00:24:31.803 --> 00:24:33.705 align:start size:94% position:6%
particularly black
conservatives today have.

00:24:33.705 --> 00:24:38.009 align:start size:84% position:16%
So that's bravery
in my book (laughs).

00:24:38.009 --> 00:24:39.177
- What kind of support

00:24:39.177 --> 00:24:41.279 align:start size:88% position:13%
do you think black
conservatives have now?

00:24:41.279 --> 00:24:43.648 align:start size:97% position:3%
Because I think you could
probably argue it both ways.

00:24:43.648 --> 00:24:47.219
On one hand, there is now some
defensive black conservatives

00:24:47.219 --> 00:24:49.187
that I have many black
conservative friends.

00:24:49.187 --> 00:24:51.823
There's more out
there obviously,

00:24:51.823 --> 00:24:53.725
but on the other hand,

00:24:53.725 --> 00:24:56.161
I'm not going to
have to tell you

00:24:56.161 --> 00:24:57.896
all the things that
you guys get called.

00:24:57.896 --> 00:24:59.164
And I don't even
want to say them

00:24:59.164 --> 00:25:01.333 align:start size:84% position:16%
because YouTube will
delete the video.

00:25:01.333 --> 00:25:04.236
- That comes with the territory.

00:25:04.236 --> 00:25:06.738
Yes. The name calling
has always been there.

00:25:06.738 --> 00:25:10.075
But there's far more support.

00:25:10.075 --> 00:25:13.445 align:start size:97% position:3%
I mean, I'll put it this
way. The way Tom has put it.

00:25:14.913 --> 00:25:18.016
Back in the 1980s, Thomas
Sowell and Walter Williams

00:25:18.016 --> 00:25:20.218
made a pact never
to fly together.

00:25:21.319 --> 00:25:22.587
Because if the plane went down,

00:25:22.587 --> 00:25:25.023
it'd be the end of black
conservatism in America.

00:25:25.023 --> 00:25:28.159 align:start size:88% position:13%
So we're a long way from
that today, you know?

00:25:28.159 --> 00:25:31.196
And just the support I get.

00:25:31.196 --> 00:25:33.999
I, you know, I was
a few years ago,

00:25:33.999 --> 00:25:36.801
I was invited to speak
at a college campus,

00:25:36.801 --> 00:25:39.638 align:start size:84% position:16%
and then I was
dis-invited to speak.

00:25:39.638 --> 00:25:41.907
I wrote a column about it.

00:25:41.907 --> 00:25:45.710
I went on television
and talked about it.

00:25:45.710 --> 00:25:49.948 align:start size:88% position:13%
The president of the
university was bombarded

00:25:49.948 --> 00:25:54.953 align:start size:97% position:3%
with complaints from graduates
and and even some students

00:25:56.121 --> 00:25:57.756
at the school for
dis-inviting me.

00:25:57.756 --> 00:26:00.592
They reversed course (laughs)

00:26:00.592 --> 00:26:02.627
and re-invited me.

00:26:02.627 --> 00:26:03.862
That sort of thing.

00:26:03.862 --> 00:26:05.964
I don't know that Tom
would have ever had

00:26:05.964 --> 00:26:09.968
that kind of support
30 or 40 years ago,

00:26:09.968 --> 00:26:14.272 align:start size:97% position:3%
but that's what's out there
today for black conservatives.

00:26:14.272 --> 00:26:15.473
Yeah.

00:26:15.473 --> 00:26:17.042 align:start size:81% position:19%
- Do you find that
his evolution,

00:26:17.042 --> 00:26:19.244
as I talked to him about it

00:26:19.244 --> 00:26:22.047
and we had this great viral
clip where I said, you know,

00:26:22.047 --> 00:26:23.982 align:start size:88% position:13%
"How did your political
evolution happen?"

00:26:23.982 --> 00:26:25.850 align:start size:84% position:16%
And, you know, he
just paused, "Facts."

00:26:25.850 --> 00:26:27.852
You know, it's just facts.

00:26:27.852 --> 00:26:29.154 align:start size:88% position:13%
That was what caused him
to go from, you know,

00:26:29.154 --> 00:26:31.456 align:start size:84% position:16%
a Marxist in essence
to a libertarian.

00:26:32.457 --> 00:26:34.059
It seems like it's happening

00:26:34.059 --> 00:26:35.560
to an awful lot of
people right now.

00:26:35.560 --> 00:26:37.896
It's not something that the
media is going to talk about,

00:26:37.896 --> 00:26:40.632
but it seems like a lot of
young people are getting it.

00:26:41.833 --> 00:26:43.802
- I, maybe.

00:26:43.802 --> 00:26:46.137 align:start size:97% position:3%
Maybe we talked to different
young people (laughs).

00:26:46.137 --> 00:26:48.039
- No, a lot of them
are missing it too.

00:26:48.039 --> 00:26:49.341 align:start size:97% position:3%
I know that.
- When I see them the rise.

00:26:50.809 --> 00:26:53.111
When I see the rise of an
Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez,

00:26:54.512 --> 00:26:56.247 align:start size:97% position:3%
the way she talks about
how their should, you know,

00:26:56.247 --> 00:26:58.016
billionaires should be banned,

00:26:58.016 --> 00:27:02.053
or that sort of thing tells me

00:27:02.053 --> 00:27:05.857 align:start size:81% position:19%
something else
might be going on.

00:27:05.857 --> 00:27:07.325
and I think, you know,

00:27:07.325 --> 00:27:09.961
part of it might have to
do with the experience.

00:27:09.961 --> 00:27:12.263
I mean, if you're a
young person today,

00:27:12.263 --> 00:27:17.268
and your idea of how, you know,
an economy should be growing

00:27:19.070 --> 00:27:22.207
or what the employment should
look like in this country,

00:27:22.207 --> 00:27:26.745
was the Obama years,
or shortly thereafter,

00:27:26.745 --> 00:27:31.616
you know, that's
your experience.

00:27:31.616 --> 00:27:34.219
And maybe you're
thinking capitalism

00:27:34.219 --> 00:27:35.553
isn't all that great of a thing.

00:27:35.553 --> 00:27:38.923 align:start size:84% position:16%
If this is, you
know, all it can do.

00:27:38.923 --> 00:27:41.259 align:start size:94% position:6%
So maybe you have a skewed
sense of capitalism,

00:27:41.259 --> 00:27:45.296
and maybe that's where
people like AOC come from

00:27:45.296 --> 00:27:48.733
in terms of how they view
free market economics.

00:27:48.733 --> 00:27:51.903
But I think that Republicans

00:27:51.903 --> 00:27:54.172 align:start size:94% position:6%
and conservative
intellectuals in general,

00:27:54.172 --> 00:27:55.674
I think need to do a better job

00:27:55.674 --> 00:27:57.909
of communicating these
ideas to young people.

00:27:57.909 --> 00:28:01.546 align:start size:84% position:16%
I think, in a large
sense, they're losing.

00:28:01.546 --> 00:28:03.181
They're losing that fight.

00:28:03.181 --> 00:28:05.617 align:start size:97% position:3%
Now the young people typically
are more left of center,

00:28:05.617 --> 00:28:07.652
as Tom was when he was younger.

00:28:08.820 --> 00:28:10.989
And then they grow up,
they grow out of it.

00:28:10.989 --> 00:28:14.492 align:start size:97% position:3%
Experience teaches them
that another way is better.

00:28:14.492 --> 00:28:17.395 align:start size:94% position:6%
But I do think that
Republicans in particular

00:28:17.395 --> 00:28:18.863
need to do a better job

00:28:18.863 --> 00:28:21.433 align:start size:97% position:3%
of communicating some of
these ideas to young people.

00:28:21.433 --> 00:28:22.901 align:start size:72% position:28%
- Yeah.
- It's funny.

00:28:22.901 --> 00:28:26.004 align:start size:97% position:3%
'Cause I hear a lot about
young people waking up to it.

00:28:26.004 --> 00:28:28.306
So that that's my own
sort of world there,

00:28:28.306 --> 00:28:30.975
but of course the
counterbalance-

00:28:30.975 --> 00:28:33.945
- And Tom has spent a career,

00:28:33.945 --> 00:28:35.980
spent a career trying
to explain these ideas.

00:28:35.980 --> 00:28:37.615
I mean, his best-selling book,

00:28:37.615 --> 00:28:40.218
he's known a lot of people
for his writings on race,

00:28:40.218 --> 00:28:43.121 align:start size:94% position:6%
but his best selling book
is "Basic Economics,"

00:28:44.289 --> 00:28:46.324
which is essentially
an economics textbook

00:28:46.324 --> 00:28:48.560
without any graphs
and equations in it.

00:28:48.560 --> 00:28:52.564
And he takes great pride
in explaining economics

00:28:52.564 --> 00:28:55.333 align:start size:97% position:3%
to non economist and everyday
people in plain English.

00:28:55.333 --> 00:28:57.902
He thinks it's a very
important thing to do.

00:28:57.902 --> 00:29:00.371 align:start size:97% position:3%
It's one of the things he
learned from Milton Friedman

00:29:00.371 --> 00:29:03.341
who spent his career
after leaving academia

00:29:03.341 --> 00:29:04.609
as a public intellectual

00:29:04.609 --> 00:29:07.245
who spent a lot of time
explaining economics

00:29:07.245 --> 00:29:11.149 align:start position:0%
to non intellectuals on
college campuses and elsewhere.

00:29:11.149 --> 00:29:14.519
And so Tom has taken great pride

00:29:14.519 --> 00:29:16.187 align:start size:84% position:16%
in doing that
throughout his career.

00:29:16.187 --> 00:29:19.124 align:start size:88% position:13%
And, you know,
sometimes in the academy

00:29:19.124 --> 00:29:21.726
that isn't exactly rewarded.

00:29:21.726 --> 00:29:23.461
It's looked down on.

00:29:23.461 --> 00:29:24.929
Why are you writing
about, you know,

00:29:24.929 --> 00:29:26.898 align:start size:94% position:6%
for the general public
instead of for your peers.

00:29:26.898 --> 00:29:29.768 align:start size:88% position:13%
But Tom thinks that
that is the proper role

00:29:29.768 --> 00:29:31.102
of a public intellectual.

00:29:31.102 --> 00:29:32.504
- Yeah, and look,

00:29:32.504 --> 00:29:34.572 align:start size:94% position:6%
as we watch public
education kind of crumble,

00:29:34.572 --> 00:29:36.174
it's like we need those voices

00:29:36.174 --> 00:29:38.610
that are gonna be
willing to do it.

00:29:38.610 --> 00:29:41.446
In knowing him, did
you ever get a sense?

00:29:41.446 --> 00:29:43.982 align:start size:97% position:3%
You know, obviously he wrote
mostly and cared mostly

00:29:43.982 --> 00:29:47.552
about economics, but there is
a lot of racial stuff there.

00:29:47.552 --> 00:29:50.655
Do you think that that was
just a by-product of the fact

00:29:50.655 --> 00:29:53.591
that he was black, so he kind
of was sort of pinned into it?

00:29:53.591 --> 00:29:57.295 align:start position:0%
Or do you think he really
wanted to talk about that stuff?

00:29:57.295 --> 00:29:59.964
I remember in our interview
trying to ask that question

00:29:59.964 --> 00:30:03.835
in a way that didn't seem
pandering or something.

00:30:03.835 --> 00:30:07.405
- No, he reluctantly came
to writing about race,

00:30:07.405 --> 00:30:10.308 align:start size:88% position:13%
and that's one of the
more interesting aspects

00:30:10.308 --> 00:30:11.509
to his background.

00:30:11.509 --> 00:30:13.912
Tom could have spent his career

00:30:13.912 --> 00:30:17.382 align:start size:94% position:6%
writing about his first
love, which was economics,

00:30:17.382 --> 00:30:21.085 align:start size:97% position:3%
and the history of economics
and the history of ideas.

00:30:21.085 --> 00:30:22.887
That is his specialty.

00:30:22.887 --> 00:30:24.656
He was an expert on Marx.

00:30:24.656 --> 00:30:27.625 align:start position:0% line:0%
He's an expert on the classical
liberals from, you know,

00:30:27.625 --> 00:30:31.095 line:0%
the Adam Smiths and John
Stuart Mills and so forth.

00:30:31.095 --> 00:30:34.332 line:0%
That was Tom's first love
writing about those guys.

00:30:34.332 --> 00:30:37.068
And that's what he spent the
beginning of his career doing.

00:30:37.068 --> 00:30:38.603
And he was good at it.

00:30:38.603 --> 00:30:41.773 align:start size:84% position:16%
I mean, he won high
praise from his peers,

00:30:41.773 --> 00:30:43.775
and he could have
stayed in that lane

00:30:43.775 --> 00:30:45.210
and lived a very fruitful life

00:30:45.210 --> 00:30:47.679 align:start size:84% position:16%
teaching and writing
about economics.

00:30:47.679 --> 00:30:50.715 align:start size:94% position:6%
He turned to writing
about race, as he puts it,

00:30:50.715 --> 00:30:52.483
out of a sense of duty.

00:30:52.483 --> 00:30:55.286
He said, "Because there are
things that need to be said."

00:30:55.286 --> 00:30:57.188
And the way he put it is,

00:30:57.188 --> 00:31:00.191 align:start size:97% position:3%
"To many other people have the
good sense not to say them.

00:31:00.191 --> 00:31:01.693
(laughing together)

00:31:01.693 --> 00:31:03.595
But they need to be said,
and someone's gotta do it."

00:31:03.595 --> 00:31:07.832
And that is why he turned
his attention to race.

00:31:07.832 --> 00:31:10.869 align:start size:97% position:3%
And he had a pretty formative
experience, I think,

00:31:10.869 --> 00:31:13.238 align:start size:84% position:16%
when he was at Cornell
in the late '60s

00:31:13.238 --> 00:31:16.307 align:start size:94% position:6%
which is when the rioting
occurred on campus.

00:31:16.307 --> 00:31:19.110
And he was there at the time.

00:31:19.110 --> 00:31:21.613
He was professor of
economics at Cornell.

00:31:21.613 --> 00:31:24.983 align:start size:97% position:3%
And I think that made
quite an impression on him,

00:31:24.983 --> 00:31:28.887 align:start size:97% position:3%
on where things were
headed, not just on campus,

00:31:28.887 --> 00:31:32.891 align:start size:97% position:3%
but off campus as well in
terms of race in this country.

00:31:32.891 --> 00:31:37.262 align:start size:97% position:3%
And I think it led ultimately
to him leaving academia

00:31:37.262 --> 00:31:39.297 align:start size:84% position:16%
and joining the
Hoover Institution,

00:31:39.297 --> 00:31:40.899
which is a think
tank at Stanford,

00:31:40.899 --> 00:31:44.402
and also led to him writing
more about racial issues,

00:31:44.402 --> 00:31:46.437
which is something he
turned his attention to

00:31:46.437 --> 00:31:47.772
in the '70s and '80s.

00:31:47.772 --> 00:31:50.375
- Do you have a sense
of how he's feeling

00:31:50.375 --> 00:31:51.910
about the state of
the world right now.

00:31:51.910 --> 00:31:54.345
I know in the last year or so,
obviously, because of COVID,

00:31:54.345 --> 00:31:56.981 align:start position:0%
he wasn't doing many interviews,
we tried to get up there,

00:31:56.981 --> 00:31:59.217 align:start size:84% position:16%
and then we just
couldn't coordinate it
with so many things.

00:31:59.217 --> 00:32:01.786
He's 91, is that right? 91?

00:32:01.786 --> 00:32:02.620 align:start size:69% position:31%
- 90.
- He's 90.

00:32:02.620 --> 00:32:04.389
So he's 90 years old.

00:32:04.389 --> 00:32:06.090
- He'll be 91 this year in June.

00:32:06.090 --> 00:32:07.292
- I mean, for 90,

00:32:07.292 --> 00:32:10.228
he is unbelievably spry
and everything else,

00:32:10.228 --> 00:32:12.263 align:start size:94% position:6%
but obviously isn't doing
as many appearances.

00:32:12.263 --> 00:32:13.665
I think he was on,

00:32:13.665 --> 00:32:16.000 align:start size:97% position:3%
I think he was on Mark
Levin's show not too long ago.

00:32:16.000 --> 00:32:19.237
Do you get a sense of
where he's at sort of,

00:32:19.237 --> 00:32:21.105 align:start size:84% position:16%
you know, in the
politics of the day?

00:32:22.307 --> 00:32:25.143
- I don't have a good sense.

00:32:25.143 --> 00:32:28.513
We've communicated
by email a few times

00:32:28.513 --> 00:32:30.348
over the past six months or so,

00:32:30.348 --> 00:32:32.483
but not a whole lot since COVID.

00:32:33.551 --> 00:32:35.954
He did publish a book last year,

00:32:35.954 --> 00:32:38.289
a brand new book,
on charter schools.

00:32:38.289 --> 00:32:40.558 align:start size:94% position:6%
And he did some
interviews for that book.

00:32:40.558 --> 00:32:42.193
So he's still working.

00:32:42.193 --> 00:32:44.195
I mean, he retired his
column a few years ago,

00:32:44.195 --> 00:32:45.463
but he's still writing books

00:32:45.463 --> 00:32:48.833
or updating editions of
old books and so forth.

00:32:48.833 --> 00:32:50.435 align:start size:84% position:16%
And I've seen him
in a few interviews.

00:32:50.435 --> 00:32:53.071
You mentioned "The
Mark Levin Show."

00:32:53.071 --> 00:32:54.539 align:start size:84% position:16%
He's done some
other shows as well.

00:32:54.539 --> 00:32:55.807
And I've seen some
of those interviews,

00:32:55.807 --> 00:32:59.043 align:start size:84% position:16%
but I don't get a
sense of where he is,

00:32:59.043 --> 00:33:00.812
how he's thinking about
what's going on now.

00:33:00.812 --> 00:33:04.015
I will say that his general,

00:33:04.015 --> 00:33:08.353 align:start size:97% position:3%
his general default position
is extreme pessimism

00:33:08.353 --> 00:33:12.991
(laughs) about the way
the world is headed.

00:33:12.991 --> 00:33:16.127 align:start position:0%
So I doubt anything that's
happened in the past year or so

00:33:16.127 --> 00:33:17.662
has changed that (laughs).

00:33:17.662 --> 00:33:21.833 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Yeah, I have a bad feeling
you're right on that one.

00:33:21.833 --> 00:33:23.267
How was it for you personally

00:33:23.267 --> 00:33:25.136 align:start size:81% position:19%
to get to know one
of your heroes?

00:33:25.136 --> 00:33:27.705
You know, they always say
don't meet you your heroes

00:33:27.705 --> 00:33:29.540 align:start size:84% position:16%
'cause it's always
going to be a let down

00:33:29.540 --> 00:33:30.775
one way or another.

00:33:30.775 --> 00:33:33.378
How was the whole
experience for you?

00:33:33.378 --> 00:33:36.881 align:start size:84% position:16%
- It was a pure joy
to spend several years

00:33:36.881 --> 00:33:41.386 align:start size:97% position:3%
delving into the intellectual
life of Tom Sowell

00:33:41.386 --> 00:33:44.188
and getting inside of his head,

00:33:44.188 --> 00:33:47.458 align:start size:97% position:3%
reading, trying to read
everything he's ever written,

00:33:47.458 --> 00:33:49.994 align:start size:88% position:13%
and he's writing so
much on so many issues.

00:33:49.994 --> 00:33:53.765
And he's just so knowledgeable.

00:33:53.765 --> 00:33:55.767
And, you know, he.

00:33:55.767 --> 00:34:00.538
Several times I found myself
while trying to write the book,

00:34:00.538 --> 00:34:03.608 align:start size:84% position:16%
trying to explain
something more clearly

00:34:03.608 --> 00:34:07.111
than he had already explained
it in what he had written.

00:34:07.111 --> 00:34:09.380
And it was,

00:34:09.380 --> 00:34:11.883
I came across that
time and time again,

00:34:11.883 --> 00:34:15.620 align:start size:81% position:19%
but it was a real,
real thrill to,

00:34:15.620 --> 00:34:19.257
and I was very happy
that he allowed me

00:34:19.257 --> 00:34:22.727 align:start size:84% position:16%
to do the book or gave
me the green light.

00:34:22.727 --> 00:34:24.829
It's not an authorized
biography, by any sense.

00:34:24.829 --> 00:34:26.764
He didn't get to see anything

00:34:26.764 --> 00:34:28.900 align:start size:88% position:13%
that I'd written before
it was published,

00:34:28.900 --> 00:34:30.401
but he did cooperate.

00:34:30.401 --> 00:34:34.138
He did sit for several long
interviews over the years.

00:34:35.273 --> 00:34:39.710
And I was just very,
very grateful for that.

00:34:41.079 --> 00:34:44.749
It's something I'm glad I did.

00:34:44.749 --> 00:34:47.018
I hope, you know,

00:34:47.018 --> 00:34:50.521
at some point I think a real
serious scholar of economics

00:34:50.521 --> 00:34:53.758 align:start size:84% position:16%
is gonna have to
delve into Tom's work

00:34:53.758 --> 00:34:55.460
and really get into the weeds,

00:34:55.460 --> 00:34:57.295 align:start size:94% position:6%
which is something a
little above my pay grade,

00:34:57.295 --> 00:34:58.996
because I'm just a journalist,

00:34:58.996 --> 00:35:03.434 align:start size:94% position:6%
but I hope I produce
something that's readable.

00:35:03.434 --> 00:35:05.470
- (laughs) I have no
doubt that you have.

00:35:05.470 --> 00:35:08.172
What about on the on
the personal side?

00:35:08.172 --> 00:35:10.174 align:start size:84% position:16%
Because I think
sometimes, you know,

00:35:10.174 --> 00:35:11.409
especially for younger people,

00:35:11.409 --> 00:35:14.312
you see these clips on
YouTube of these people,

00:35:14.312 --> 00:35:17.548 align:start size:84% position:16%
or you see them in
the box on Fox or CNN,

00:35:17.548 --> 00:35:19.917
and you kind of almost don't
think they're real people.

00:35:19.917 --> 00:35:23.754 align:start size:88% position:13%
Like they're just these
things that exist.

00:35:23.754 --> 00:35:25.189
You know, they're almost
like cartoon characters

00:35:25.189 --> 00:35:26.457
of themselves in a way.

00:35:26.457 --> 00:35:29.160
What did you learn about
just the way he's wired

00:35:29.160 --> 00:35:33.131
or his temperament
or just interesting
on the personal side?

00:35:33.131 --> 00:35:36.400 align:start size:97% position:3%
'Cause I don't know that
people know much of anything.

00:35:36.400 --> 00:35:37.802
- Yeah. I'll start with.

00:35:39.437 --> 00:35:42.039
I use a story in the book to try

00:35:42.039 --> 00:35:44.876
and explain Tom's personality.

00:35:44.876 --> 00:35:47.812
And I use a joke
from an old movie.

00:35:47.812 --> 00:35:50.715 align:start size:84% position:16%
There was an old
movie called "Sabrina"

00:35:50.715 --> 00:35:52.416
starring Harrison
Ford that came out,

00:35:52.416 --> 00:35:53.918
I think, in the '90s sometime.

00:35:55.820 --> 00:35:59.357 align:start size:84% position:16%
And the love
interest in the movie

00:36:00.758 --> 00:36:05.563 align:start position:0%
is Harrison Ford plays this
very curmudgeonly industrialist.

00:36:06.764 --> 00:36:10.067
And the family has a
long time chauffeur.

00:36:10.067 --> 00:36:11.669
And the daughter
of the chauffeur

00:36:11.669 --> 00:36:13.471
falls for Harrison
Ford's character.

00:36:13.471 --> 00:36:15.506
And she goes to her dad
one day, and she says,

00:36:15.506 --> 00:36:19.277
"Dad what was Harrison Ford
like when he was younger?"

00:36:19.277 --> 00:36:22.580
And her father looks at
her and says, "Shorter."

00:36:22.580 --> 00:36:23.748
(laughing together)

00:36:23.748 --> 00:36:25.716
- I think I got the idea here.

00:36:26.884 --> 00:36:30.121 align:start size:84% position:16%
- And it's a way of
just saying Tom Sowell

00:36:30.121 --> 00:36:33.124
has been Thom Sowell for a
very, very, very long time.

00:36:33.124 --> 00:36:36.060 align:start size:84% position:16%
And if you go back and
read or talk to him

00:36:36.060 --> 00:36:39.730
about, you know, arguments
he would have for people

00:36:39.730 --> 00:36:42.066
when he was in grade
school or high school,

00:36:43.501 --> 00:36:46.537 align:start size:97% position:3%
very much like the arguments
he has with people today.

00:36:46.537 --> 00:36:51.175
He's someone who
has been, sort of,

00:36:51.175 --> 00:36:53.911
ready for intellectual combat,

00:36:53.911 --> 00:36:56.547
it seems like since
the day he was born.

00:36:56.547 --> 00:37:00.585
He's a very serious person.

00:37:00.585 --> 00:37:03.454
He doesn't suffer fools,

00:37:03.454 --> 00:37:07.658
but he's also a very generous
person, a very kind person.

00:37:07.658 --> 00:37:12.663
And I got that from a lot
of people I interviewed.

00:37:13.531 --> 00:37:14.799
Tom would go to bat with them

00:37:14.799 --> 00:37:16.367
when they came to
him with a problem

00:37:16.367 --> 00:37:20.204
whether it was a colleague,
a student, someone like that,

00:37:20.204 --> 00:37:22.173
Tom was willing to
go to bat for them.

00:37:23.808 --> 00:37:25.977 align:start size:84% position:16%
And so that side of
him also came through,

00:37:25.977 --> 00:37:28.579
but he's always
been a, you know,

00:37:28.579 --> 00:37:32.917 align:start size:84% position:16%
a very intellectually
curious person

00:37:32.917 --> 00:37:37.922
and someone who was pretty
confident of his abilities.

00:37:40.258 --> 00:37:44.195
And, you know, he's
a former Marine,

00:37:44.195 --> 00:37:46.197 align:start size:84% position:16%
which might surprise
a lot of people,

00:37:46.197 --> 00:37:47.965
because he's also
a bit of a loner.

00:37:47.965 --> 00:37:49.800
He's not much of a team player.

00:37:49.800 --> 00:37:52.236
And I don't think those
attributes work very well

00:37:52.236 --> 00:37:54.472
in the military (laughs).

00:37:54.472 --> 00:37:56.841 align:start size:97% position:3%
- And I know for a fact.
- Somehow, he made it though.

00:37:56.841 --> 00:37:58.743 align:start size:94% position:6%
- I know for a fact, he's
not much of a techie,

00:37:58.743 --> 00:38:01.512
because we were trying to
get Skype on his computer

00:38:01.512 --> 00:38:05.016 align:start size:97% position:3%
so we could do it during
COVID, but he didn't want it.

00:38:05.016 --> 00:38:06.517
He didn't want to download it.

00:38:06.517 --> 00:38:08.619 align:start size:97% position:3%
And then we were going to
literally deliver a computer

00:38:08.619 --> 00:38:10.888
to his door that he would
have just had to open,

00:38:10.888 --> 00:38:13.357
but a whole other situation.

00:38:13.357 --> 00:38:14.825
You'll, you know,
one of the other-

00:38:14.825 --> 00:38:19.196 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Yeah, he's not a people
person in that sense.

00:38:19.196 --> 00:38:22.500 align:start size:88% position:13%
He's worked from home
for a long, a long time.

00:38:22.500 --> 00:38:25.369
He has an office on
the Stanford campus,

00:38:25.369 --> 00:38:28.673
but generally he would go in

00:38:28.673 --> 00:38:30.608 align:start size:84% position:16%
just to do interviews
with people,

00:38:30.608 --> 00:38:32.977
but mostly work from home, yeah.

00:38:32.977 --> 00:38:34.211
- You know, it's so interesting

00:38:34.211 --> 00:38:35.813
because right now
when we all talk

00:38:35.813 --> 00:38:37.048
about the sort of
lack of discourse

00:38:37.048 --> 00:38:38.983
and the lack of sort of
honest public debate,

00:38:38.983 --> 00:38:40.484
and it was like for years,

00:38:40.484 --> 00:38:42.586
he was debating all sorts
of people on "Firing Line,"

00:38:42.586 --> 00:38:46.957
all these other shows and and
public events and all of that.

00:38:46.957 --> 00:38:49.760
And I would guess that
if they're, you know,

00:38:49.760 --> 00:38:51.762 align:start size:97% position:3%
I'm sure he loves the writing
that he's still doing,

00:38:51.762 --> 00:38:55.533
but I'm guessing he misses
probably some level of that,

00:38:55.533 --> 00:38:57.735 align:start size:88% position:13%
the public sparring
with some of the people.

00:38:58.836 --> 00:39:00.104
- I guess so,

00:39:00.104 --> 00:39:01.706
but, you know, like you
said, he's 90 years old.

00:39:01.706 --> 00:39:04.408
He's been doing it
for a long time.

00:39:04.408 --> 00:39:08.746 align:start size:97% position:3%
I think he said his piece
more than a few dozen times.

00:39:08.746 --> 00:39:10.481
- He doesn't need
that last knockout?

00:39:10.481 --> 00:39:12.149
He doesn't need that last round?

00:39:12.149 --> 00:39:14.485
- Yeah, you know, I
remember asking him

00:39:14.485 --> 00:39:16.520
after he retired the column.

00:39:16.520 --> 00:39:19.423 align:start size:84% position:16%
We were having
dinner, and I said,

00:39:19.423 --> 00:39:22.026
"Do you miss being able to
get things off your chest?"

00:39:22.026 --> 00:39:23.494
And he said,

00:39:23.494 --> 00:39:26.263
"No, there's nothing left
on my chest." (laughs)

00:39:26.263 --> 00:39:28.899
I think he, you know,

00:39:28.899 --> 00:39:33.904 align:start size:97% position:3%
Sowell put a lot of thought
and time into his work,

00:39:35.306 --> 00:39:39.343
and that required, you
know, doing your homework

00:39:39.343 --> 00:39:42.346 align:start size:94% position:6%
and staying up on current
events and so forth.

00:39:42.346 --> 00:39:44.081 align:start size:84% position:16%
And I think not having
to do that anymore.

00:39:44.081 --> 00:39:47.218
I mean, he mentioned right
before he retired his column,

00:39:47.218 --> 00:39:48.486
or decided to retire it,

00:39:48.486 --> 00:39:50.788
that he had gone on a
sort of a photo safari

00:39:50.788 --> 00:39:52.189
with a group of friends.

00:39:52.189 --> 00:39:54.925 align:start size:97% position:3%
He's an amateur photographer
and a very good one

00:39:54.925 --> 00:39:56.360
and has been for a long time.

00:39:56.360 --> 00:39:59.697 align:start size:97% position:3%
But he ended up a few buddies
went on a photo outing,

00:39:59.697 --> 00:40:01.232
a multiple day one,

00:40:01.232 --> 00:40:02.733
and they, you know,

00:40:02.733 --> 00:40:06.370 align:start size:97% position:3%
they didn't have any access
to radio or TV for a few days.

00:40:06.370 --> 00:40:09.173 align:start size:97% position:3%
And he recalled how pleasant
that experience was.

00:40:09.173 --> 00:40:12.176 align:start size:94% position:6% line:0%
And, you know, he was
already well into his 80s.

00:40:12.176 --> 00:40:13.811 line:0%
And he said, "You know,

00:40:13.811 --> 00:40:17.114 line:0%
I think I'm ready to (laughs)
tune out of the day to day."

00:40:17.114 --> 00:40:19.984
And so I think he's
probably enjoying it.

00:40:19.984 --> 00:40:21.919 align:start size:94% position:6%
I mean, like I said, he's
still doing the books

00:40:21.919 --> 00:40:24.622 align:start size:97% position:3%
and staying active
intellectually on that front,

00:40:24.622 --> 00:40:25.923 align:start size:84% position:16%
but I don't think
he misses, probably,

00:40:25.923 --> 00:40:28.392 align:start size:88% position:13%
turning out that column
or two every week.

00:40:28.392 --> 00:40:30.394 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Yeah. You'll probably
appreciate this.

00:40:30.394 --> 00:40:32.730
When we sat down at Stanford,

00:40:32.730 --> 00:40:35.132 align:start size:81% position:19%
his guys said he's
got 45 minutes.

00:40:35.132 --> 00:40:36.901
Like he doesn't want to
sit there for makeup.

00:40:36.901 --> 00:40:40.471
It's like when he gets in,
sit down, start 45 minutes.

00:40:40.471 --> 00:40:42.039
So I was very aware.

00:40:42.039 --> 00:40:44.975
I did not want to take one
extra minute of his time.

00:40:44.975 --> 00:40:47.011
And when we hit 45, I ended it,

00:40:47.011 --> 00:40:49.480 align:start size:97% position:3%
and he looked at me the
second the camera went off,

00:40:49.480 --> 00:40:51.649
and he goes, "Is that all?"

00:40:51.649 --> 00:40:54.385 align:start size:63% position:38%
Like.
(laughs)

00:40:54.385 --> 00:40:55.853
I think he said, "Is that all?

00:40:55.853 --> 00:40:57.788 align:start size:94% position:6%
We just started," or
something to that effect.

00:40:57.788 --> 00:41:01.225
And I thought, "Man, if
I would've known that,

00:41:01.225 --> 00:41:03.060
I would've kept going,"

00:41:03.060 --> 00:41:04.395
but I wanted to
be respectful of,

00:41:04.395 --> 00:41:07.698
you know, that he's busy
and all of those things.

00:41:07.698 --> 00:41:10.835 align:start position:0%
Well, listen, I am super psyched
obviously to watch the doc,

00:41:10.835 --> 00:41:12.203
and I'm honored
to be part of it.

00:41:12.203 --> 00:41:14.705
And I can't wait to
get the book as well.

00:41:14.705 --> 00:41:17.975
Is there anything else that
you'd want people to know

00:41:17.975 --> 00:41:21.745
before they partake in the
doc or pick up the book?

00:41:23.080 --> 00:41:24.815
- No, I think you've covered it.

00:41:24.815 --> 00:41:25.649
I think it,

00:41:26.817 --> 00:41:28.452
I think both the book
and the documentary

00:41:28.452 --> 00:41:30.955 align:start size:84% position:16%
is a nice introduction
to Thomas Sowell.

00:41:30.955 --> 00:41:34.391
And then I hope people, I
hope I wet their appetite,

00:41:34.391 --> 00:41:37.495
and they go read more of
them, because like I said,

00:41:37.495 --> 00:41:40.231
and like you, like you said,

00:41:40.231 --> 00:41:41.732
he's been right for a long time,

00:41:41.732 --> 00:41:44.635 align:start size:97% position:3%
and his stuff is as relevant
today as it's ever been.

00:41:44.635 --> 00:41:46.737
So we'll see what happens.

00:41:46.737 --> 00:41:48.005
- Awesome.

00:41:48.005 --> 00:41:49.206
Well, Jason, I'm glad
we finally got to talk,

00:41:49.206 --> 00:41:52.243 align:start size:88% position:13%
and should we send
people to your Twitter?

00:41:52.243 --> 00:41:53.511
Should we send them
to your Twitter?

00:41:53.511 --> 00:41:55.446
- That's fine. You can
send people my Twitter.

00:41:55.446 --> 00:41:56.947
- Is it Jason Riley?

00:41:56.947 --> 00:41:58.582
No, there's a letter in there
somewhere, right? I don't.

00:41:58.582 --> 00:42:00.150
- It's @JasonRileyWSJ.

00:42:00.150 --> 00:42:01.385
- WSJ, that's it. Okay.

00:42:01.385 --> 00:42:02.887
I knew it was in
there somewhere.

00:42:02.887 --> 00:42:04.388
All right. Well, it was a
pleasure to have you on.

00:42:04.388 --> 00:42:06.423
I can't wait to see the doc,
and I'll talk to you soon.

00:42:06.423 --> 00:42:07.358
- Thank you.

00:42:07.358 --> 00:42:08.559
Take care.

00:42:08.559 --> 00:42:09.860 align:start size:84% position:16%
- If you're looking
for more honest

00:42:09.860 --> 00:42:11.195 align:start size:97% position:3%
and thoughtful
conversations about politics

00:42:11.195 --> 00:42:12.596
instead of nonstop yelling,

